<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Places I Can Go</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm at School.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Interpret short, simple messages from class texts. Communication: Assist peers in following oral directions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can interpret short, simple messages about locations using gestures (e.g., points to "hospital" when hearing "I'm at the hospital."). Can assist peers in following directions by pointing or using gestures (e.g., points to "home" on a map). Can guide peers by demonstrating simple actions to show locations (e.g., pointing to "park" and saying "This is the park.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and initiates a quick review of location vocabulary using flashcards for "home," "school," "market," "park," "hospital." The teacher holds up a flashcard and asks, "Where is this?" Students respond chorally, "It's the hospital!" The teacher then asks, "Where are you now?" Students say, "I'm at school!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a simple map (similar to the project map) with "home," "school," and "park" labeled. The teacher points to "home" and says, "This is my home. I'm at home." Then the teacher points to "school" and says, "This is the school. I'm at school. The school is near my home." The teacher then models assisting: "If my friend asks, 'Where is the park?', I point and say, 'This is the park.'" The teacher emphasizes clear pronunciation of /sk/ in "school" and /m/ in "market" while modeling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I point to the park, am I helping my friend?" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I'm at school' and point to school, am I telling about the place?" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Places I Can Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large, simple illustrated "Community Map" on the board or projector, depicting "home," "school," "market," "park," "library," "hospital." Each location has a small speech bubble or label with simple phrases like "I'm at home," "I'm at the market," "Go to the park." (Genre: Simple pictorial map with short messages/instructions; Register: Informal; Format: Visual with text labels).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students interpret short, simple messages about locations from a visual text and assist in following simple directions. They also recognize polite expressions for confirming locations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> The teacher asks, "What places do you see on the map?" and points to different locations. Students name the places. "Where is the boy in the picture?" (pointing to a character bubble saying "I'm at school").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> The teacher reads aloud a phrase like "I'm at the library." Students point to the library on the map. The teacher might say "Show me the market" (emphasizing /m/ in "market"), and students point.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher makes statements based on the map. For example, "The hospital is next to the park. True or False?" Students indicate thumbs up/down or say "True" or "False."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Interpreting locations from text and assisting peers with directions using gestures and simple sentences ("I'm at...", "It's near...", "This is..."). Grammar: Contractions (I'm), Prepositions of place (at, near, next to). Vocabulary: home, school, market, park, library, hospital, pharmacy, bakery, restaurant. Pronunciation of /sk/ and /m/ and blending of simple phonemes (e.g., /ɪn/ for "in"). Pragmatic: Describing/confirming locations. Sociolinguistic: Polite expressions for responding.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Interpret and Match:</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with simple sentences like "I'm at the hospital," "I'm at the park," and small pictures of the corresponding locations. Students draw a line to match the sentence to the correct picture. The teacher circulates, checking for correct interpretation and pronunciation of words like "hospital."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Point and Say:</b> Students work in small groups with a shared simple map. The teacher calls out a place, e.g., "Go to the market!" One student in the group points to the "market" on the map, and another student says, "This is the market. I'm at the market!" The teacher encourages the use of polite expressions, e.g., "Excuse me, where is the pharmacy?" and polite responses, "It's here! Thank you."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Guided Direction:</b> Students work in pairs, each with a copy of a simple map. Student A tells Student B where to go (e.g., "Go to the school, please."). Student B points to the school on their map and says, "This is the school." Then they swap roles. The focus is on assisting the peer through gestures (pointing) and simple verbal confirmation.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Places I Can Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Project Presentation & Peer Mediation:</b> Students, in small groups (3-4 students), present their "Where Am I? Map" project. Each student takes turns explaining their map and then mediates for their peers by interpreting directions or clarifying locations on each other's maps.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Individual Map Presentation:</b> Each student briefly presents their "Where Am I? Map" project to their small group. They point to "home," "school," and "market" and say, "This is my home. I'm at home. This is the school. I'm at school. The school is next to my home." (This incorporates "I'm at..." sentences and prepositions.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Peer Mediation & Interpretation:</b> After each presentation, group members take turns asking questions or giving simple directions related to the map.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A (map owner) presents their map.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B asks: "Excuse me, where is the market on your map?" (using a polite expression).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A points to the market on their map and says, "The market is here. It's near my home." (Assisting peers, guiding, confirming locations).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student C might say, "Show me your park," and Student A points and says, "This is the park."</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Guidance:</b> The teacher might give a simple scenario for the group, e.g., "You are at home. How do you go to the library?" Students in the group collaboratively use their maps and gestures to explain, e.g., "First, walk to the school. Then, the library is next to the school." (Encouraging collective mediation and use of target vocabulary/grammar).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: Guided Map Tour:</b> In pairs, students use their "Where Am I? Map" project. Student A gives a simple oral direction (e.g., "Go to the park, please.") or asks about a location. Student B interprets the direction/question and points to the correct location on their map, then verbalizes the location ("This is the park. I'm at the park."). They switch roles after 2-3 turns. The teacher observes specific instances of mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interpretation:</b> Can interpret simple oral messages about locations (points correctly).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Assistance/Guidance:</b> Uses pointing/gestures to assist peers.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Language Use:</b> Uses "I'm at..." and prepositions of place (at, near, next to).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Uses target location vocabulary (home, school, market, park, etc.).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Pronounces /sk/ and /m/ intelligibly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Actively assists and guides their peer.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I help my friend find places on a map?" (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I tell about my home and school on my map?" (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy about helping your friend with the map and directions?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult when you tried to tell your friend about places?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in facilitating student mediation and using their project maps? (e.g., peer interaction, visual aids, clear instructions).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for future lessons, especially for students who struggled with interpreting or guiding? (e.g., more basic map exercises, explicit gesture modeling, sentence frames for asking/answering directions).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "I'm at School" and the "Where Am I? Map" project. The next lesson will begin a new theme, building upon the foundational communication skills developed in this unit.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>